Organic–inorganic lead iodide perovskites with a chemical formula of $\mathsf{A P b l}_{3}$ (where A is organic cation) have recently emerged as promising materials for photovoltaic applications.[1] Till now, methylammonium $(C H_{3}N H_{3}+)$ and formamidinium $((N H_{2})_{2}C H^{+})$ are two dominated organic cations used in perovskite solar cells (PSCs), which produce three-dimensional (3D) perovskites with suitable band gaps $(<1.7\ {\mathsf{e V}})$ for solar cell applications.[2] However, the small organic cations (i.e. $C H_{3}N H_{3}+$ and $(N H_{2})_{2}C H^{+})$ with hydrophilic nature lead to the instability of these perovskites and thus hinder their commercialization.[3] Thus, there is a strong desire to explore new stable perovskites with suitable photovoltaic properties.
Recently, there are many attempts to substitute $C H_{3}N H_{3}+$ and $(N H_{2})_{2}C H^{+}$ with large organic cations to enhance the stability of perovskites.[4] However, this approach faces two challenges. Firstly, large organic cations usually result in structure distortion and form low-dimensional structures with much reduced electrical conductivities.[5] Secondly, large organic cations often broaden bandgap of the perovskite materials (e.g. bandgap of EtNH3PbI3 is $\sim2.2\ \mathsf{e V}$ , $E_{\mathsf{t N H}_{3}+}$ is ethylammonium).[6] The large bandgaps limit the absorption in long wavelength, and typically resulting in lower power conversion efficiency $(P C E)$ of $<3\%$ .[6c, 7] Till now, there are no reports on large organic cation-based perovskites with suitable bandgaps for solar cell applications. For the first time, a new 3D perovskite with a chemical structure of $(n{-}\mathsf{P r}(\mathsf{N H}_{3})_{2})_{0.5}\mathsf{P b l}_{3}$ is prepared based on a larger organic cation of propane-1,3-diammonium $(n-P r(N H_{3})_{2}^{2+})$ . X-ray diffraction (XRD) result shows that the new perovskite exhibits a tetragonal structure. Furthermore, the $(n{-}\mathsf{P r}(\mathsf{N H}_{3})_{2})_{0.5}\mathsf{P b l}_{3}$ perovskite shows a narrow bandgap of $\sim\ 1.6\ \mathsf{e V}$ , which is suitable for solar cell application. Our first-generation (n$P r(N H_{3})_{2})_{0.5}P b|_{3}P S C$ yields a $P C E$ of $5.1\%$ . This new perovskite shows enhanced stability under high humidity and high temperature comparing to the standard ${\mathsf{C H}}_{3}{\mathsf{N H}}_{3}{\mathsf{P b l}}_{3}$ perovskite.
Perovskite thin film (i.e. $(n{\cdot}\mathsf{P r}(\mathsf{N H_{3}})_{2})_{0.5}\mathsf{P b l_{3}})$ was fabricated by a one-step spin coating method. Figure 1a shows that the XRD pattern of the $(n{\cdot}\mathsf{P r}(\mathsf{N H_{3}})_{2})_{0{.}5}\mathsf{P b l_{3}}$ perovskite exhibits 4 peaks corresponding to (002), (110), (004), and (220) reflections of the tetragonal perovskite structure.[8] No observable peak corresponding to low-dimensional structure is found.[9] To further confirm the strucuture, powder XRD (Figure S1) is conducted and the peaks at 13.9, 14.3, 19.7, 20.1, 23.7, 24.7, 28.0, and $28.6^{\circ}$ are indexed to the (002), (110), (112), (200), (211), (202), (004), and (220) planes of tetragonal perovskite structure, respectively.[10] Figure 1b shows an absorption and a photoluminescence (PL) spectra of the $(n\mathrm{-}\mathsf{P r}(\mathsf{N H_{3}})_{2})_{0.5}\mathsf{P b l_{3}}$ perovskite. The estimation bandgap from the long wavelength absorption cutoff gives a value of $\sim1.62\mathsf{e V}$ .
Perovskites with large organic cations usually adopt chemical formula of $\mathsf{A P b}|_{3}$ or $\mathsf{A}_{2}\mathsf{P b l}_{3}$ and the A/ Pb ratio is $\sim100$ and $200\%$ respectively, resulting in lattice expansion and formation of low-dimensional structure.[4b, 11] Owing to quantum confinement effect in low-dimensional perovskite, a blue shifted absorption with relatively large bandgaps $(>2\ {\mathsf{e V}})$ is usually observed. However, in $(n\mathrm{-}\mathsf{P r}(\mathsf{N H_{3}})_{2})_{0.5}\mathsf{P b l_{3}}$ , the $\therefore n-P r(N H_{3})_{2}/$ Pb” ratio of $\sim50\%$ is much smaller than previous reported system due to the diammonium cation (i.e. $\mathsf{n}{\mathsf{-P r}}({\mathsf{N H}}_{3})_{2})$ , and thus 3D structure without low-dimensional structure is observed. The bandgap of the new perovskite is similar to the ${\mathsf{C H}}_{3}{\mathsf{N H}}_{3}{\mathsf{P b l}}_{3}$ perovskite $(\sim1.59\ \mathsf{e V})$ .[12] To the best of our knowledge, this is the first report of large organic cation-based 3D perovskites with bandgap $<1.7\mathsf{e V}$ .[13]
To investigate the photovoltaic performance of the (n$\mathsf{P r}(\mathsf{N H}_{3})_{2})_{0.5}\mathsf{P b l}_{3}$ perovskite, solar cells were fabricated with a configuration of ITO/ poly(3,4-ethylenedioxythiophene): poly(styrenesulfonate) (PEDOT: PSS)/perovskites $\scriptstyle{\mathcal{C}}_{60}/2,9.$ - dimethyl-4,7-diphenyl-1,10-phenanthroline (BCP)/Ag and measured under standard AM1.5G illumination. Current density– voltage (J–V) curve and external quantum efficiency (EQE) of the optimized device are shown in Figure 2. The champion PSC exhibits a $P C E$ of $5.1\%$ (statistics of PCEs is also included in Figure S2), with an open-circuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{{oc}}})$ of $1.06\mathrm{~V~}$ , a shortcircuit current $(J_{\mathsf{s c}})$ of $7.5~\mathsf{m A c m^{-2}}$ and a fill factor $(F F)$ of $63.8\%$ . The EQE integrated $J_{\mathsf{s c}}$ is $7.5~\mathsf{m A c m^{-2}}$ and is consistent with the $\mathsf{J-V}$ obtained result. Electrochemical impedance spectra (EIS) of $(n-P r(N H_{3})_{2})_{0.5}P b|_{3}$ and $C H_{3}N H_{3}P b l_{3}$ PSCs $(30^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ in Figure S3 show that the charge transport resistance $(R_{\mathrm{ct}})$ of the (n$P r(N H_{3})_{2})_{0.5}P b|_{3}$ perovskite is 189 ohm $\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ , which is larger than that of $C H_{3}N H_{3}P O l_{3}$ perovskite (109 ohm cm-2). This inferior charge transport leads to the reduced $J_{s c}$ and low PCE of the n$\mathsf{P r}(\mathsf{N H}_{3})_{2})_{0.5}\mathsf{P b l}_{3}$ PSC.
Stability of the $(n{\cdot}\mathsf{P r}(\mathsf{N H_{3}})_{2})_{0{.}5}\mathsf{P b l_{3}}$ PSC was further investigated. Figure 3a shows the normalized PCE of devices without encapsulation as a function of storage duration. After each I–V measurement, the PSC was stored under $85\%$ relative humidity (RH) at room temperature (RT) in dark. The stability results of the ${\mathsf{C H}}_{3}{\mathsf{N H}}_{3}{\mathsf{P b l}}_{3}$ reference PSC under the same conditions are also shown for comparison. In Figure 3a, PCE of CH3NH3PbI3 reference PSC decreases to $20\%$ of its original value after $\sim240$ h, which is comparable to the results of perovskite stored at similar condition.[14]. In contrast, $(n\mathrm{-}\mathsf{P r}(\mathsf{N H_{3}})_{2})_{0.5}\mathsf{P b l_{3}}$ PSC can retain over $60\%$ of its original $P C E$ after $300\mathrm{~h~}$ . The enhanced stability of ( ${n-P r(N H_{3})_{2})_{0.5}}P b_{3}$ perovskite is attributed to the large hydrophobic cations (i.e. $n{\cdot}\mathsf{P r}(\mathsf{N H}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}^{2+})$ .[4c]
The above experiment was repeated at $80^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ storage condition (i.e. thermal stability). As shown in Figure 3b, PCE of the (n$P r(N H_{3})_{2})_{0.5}P b|_{3}$ PSC still retains $90\%$ of its initial efficiency after $30\ \mathsf{h}$ which is much higher than that of the $\mathsf{C H}_{3}\mathsf{N H}_{3}\mathsf{P b l}_{3}$ reference PSC. To explain the better thermal stability, we follow the EIS approach of Bag et al. and activation energy $(\mathsf{E}_{\mathsf{a}})$ for thermal-induced ion diffusion is determined with the equation:[15]
$$
\mathrm{\frac{1}{T w}}=\mathrm{\frac{kT}{h}}\frac{a^{2}\upalpha}{{\mathrm{L}}_{\mathrm{D}}^{2}}\mathrm{exp}(\mathrm{\frac{-E_{a}}{k T}})
$$
where, TW is the Warburg time constant and is from the fitting results of EIS at various temperatures (Figure S3), h is Planck’s constant, a is the lattice parameter, α is the coordination factor, LD is perovskite film thickness. By plotting ln[1/(T·Tw)] vs 1/T as shown in Figure 3c, we calculate that $\mathsf{E}_{\mathsf{a}}$ of ${\mathsf{C H}}_{3}{\mathsf{N H}}_{3}{\mathsf{P b l}}_{3}$ perovskite is $49k J\cdot\mathsf{m o l}^{-1}$ in the temperature region $(<320~\mathsf{K})$ and $11\ \mathsf{k J}\cdot\ \mathsf{m o l}^{-1}$ in the ${>}320~\mathsf{K}$ region.[16] The different activation energies at different temperature region are attributed to phase transition.[17] For $(n\mathrm{-}\mathsf{P r}(\mathsf{N H_{3}})_{2})_{0.5}\mathsf{P b l_{3}}$ perovskite, $\mathsf{E}_{\mathsf{a}}$ is $73\mathsf{k J}$ · mol-1 at all temperatures, indicating that no phase transition happens. It was reported that the diffusion of the organic cations can lead to the degradation of perovskite at high temperature.[17] The high $\mathsf{E}_{\mathsf{a}}$ in $(n\mathrm{-}\mathsf{P r}(\mathsf{N H_{3}})_{2})_{0.5}\mathsf{P b l_{3}}$ perovskite indicates an increasing threshold for ion diffusion, leading to the enhanced thermal stability. Two main reasons can lead to the inhibited ion diffusion in $(n\mathrm{-}\mathsf{P r}(\mathsf{N H_{3}})_{2})_{0.5}\mathsf{P b l_{3}}$ perovskite: first, the $(n\mathrm{-}\mathsf{P r}(\mathsf{N H_{3}})_{2})_{0.5}\mathsf{P b l_{3}}$ perovskite is organic cation depleted structure with $\"n-\mathsf{P r}(\mathsf{N H}_{3})_{2}/$ ${\sf P b}^{\prime\prime}$ ratio of $\sim50\%$ , which can reduce the possibility of organic cation diffusion; second, the large organic cations (i.e. n$\mathsf{P r}(\mathsf{N H}_{3})_{2})$ can inhibit the ion diffusion.[18]
In summary, we demonstrate a new large organic cation-based perovskite with 3D structure and suitable bandgap for solar cell applications. Our $(n\mathrm{-}\mathsf{P r}(\mathsf{N H_{3}})_{2})_{0.5}\mathsf{P b l_{3}}$ PSC shows a PCE of \~ $5.1\ \%$ and exhibits enhanced stability under high humidity and high temperature compared to ${\mathsf{C H}}_{3}{\mathsf{N H}}_{3}{\mathsf{P b l}}_{3}$ . $(n{-}\mathsf{P r}(\mathsf{N H_{3}})_{2})_{0.5}\mathsf{P b l_{3}}$ PSC without encapsulation can retain over $60\%$ of its efficiency upon storage for over $300\mathrm{~h~}$ under $85\%$ RH and can maintain $90\%$ of their initial efficiency at $80^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for over 30 h. The enhanced stability is attributed to the large organic cations, which can prevent the direct exposure to moisture and inhibit the thermal-induced ion diffusion.
## Experimental Section
Fabrication of devices: 0.5 mmol propane-1,3-diammonium iodine (Greatcellsolar), 1 mmol of lead iodide (Sigma-Aldrich) were dissolved in 1 mL of anhydrous N, N-dimethylformamide (Sigma-Aldrich) with 1.25 mmol of dimethylsulfoxide (SigmaAldrich) and 0.7 mmol of methylammonium chloride (SigmaAldrich). The solutions were stirred at room temperature for more than $2h$ . Solar cells have a configuration of ITO/PEDOT: PSS/perovskite $\slash{\sf C}_{60}$ (20 nm)/BCP (8 nm)/Ag (70 nm). Indium tin oxide coated glass substrates were cleaned routinely. PEDOT: PSS films were fabricated by spin coating at a speed of 3000 rpm followed by heating at $140^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for 10 mins. Then the samples were transferred into a ${\mathsf N}_{2}$ -filled glovebox. Perovskite precursor solutions were spin coated at 5000 rpm followed by solvent annealing at $100^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for 10 mins. This heating process is optimized, as illustrated in Figure S4 and Table 1. Finally, devices were finished by thermal deposition of $\mathsf{C}_{60}$ , BCP (Lumtec) and Ag electrode. The active device area is $0.1\ \mathsf{c m}^{2}$ . Crystalline $(n\mathrm{-}\mathsf{P r}(\mathsf{N H_{3}})_{2})_{0.5}\mathsf{P b l_{3}}$ was recovered from the glass after cooling.
Characterization: XRD results were measured with a Philips X’ Pert diffractometer with Cu Kα radiation. Absorption spectra were recorded using a PerkinElmer model Lambda 2S UV−vis spectrometer. The PL were obtained with a FLS 980 spectrofluorometer (Edingburgh Instruments Ltd.) J-V curves of the PSCs were measured with Keithley source measurement unit under $100\mathrm{\mw\cm^{-2}}$ with an Oriel 150 W solar simulator. Scan rate is $50\mathrm{mV/s}$ . EIS results were measured with a ZAHNER IM6 workstation with a frequency range from 1 Hz to $10^{6}~\mathsf{H z}$ under open circuit condition in the dark. The obtained spectra were fitted by Z-View software.
